{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Anammox in the Dark: Black Sea Redoxcline Nitrogen Loss",
    "date": "2026-07-26",
    "lesson": "In the permanently anoxic Black Sea, a thin band around 80-120m depth hosts anammox bacteria (Candidatus Scalindua) that combine ammonium and nitrite directly into nitrogen gas, without oxygen or sunlight. This single reaction removes a huge fraction of the ocean's fixed nitrogen, acting as a planetary safety valve on eutrophication. Anammox cells are chemolithoautotrophs: they fix carbon from the energy of this reaction rather than eating organic food, and they only thrive where their two chemical reactants overlap in a narrow depth band - too shallow and there is no nitrite/ammonium accumulation, too deep and substrate runs out. This level asks you to find and hold that band, in cold, dim, sluggish water where almost everything sinks and nothing grows fast.",
    "citation": "Kuypers et al. 2003 PNAS; Ward et al. 2009 - Black Sea anammox and nitrogen loss",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Black Sea redoxcline anammox (Candidatus Scalindua), ~80-120m depth"
  },
